KV read cost for the DSpark paper scheduler, on top of
vllm-apertus-dspark-draft-cut.patch (apply the confidence-verify, paper-scheduler
and draft-cut patches first). Enabled with VLLM_DSPARK_KV_COST=1 next to
VLLM_DSPARK_VERIFY_MODE=paper.

The cost table is profiled with short contexts, as the paper assumes context
length is marginal. Under load it is not: every step's attention streams each
scheduled request's whole KV once, whatever is verified (~7 ms per step at
C=256 on the held-out prompts, 230k context tokens, ~32 us per 1,000 tokens on
GH200; the residual step time of every arm, plain included). Without it the
draft-cut scheduler sees short steps as cheaper than they are and at C=256 cuts
to one slot, taking 1.5x the steps of k=3 and falling 7% behind it (jobs
3670246/7). The step now costs kv_ms_per_token * sum(context) more, with
kv_ms_per_token profiled at engine start (target KV bytes per token over the
measured HBM read bandwidth) or set with VLLM_DSPARK_KV_MS_PER_KTOK (ms per
1,000 tokens).

With the draft cut, the scheduler also logs every 500 steps the measured step
interval against the table's prediction (without the KV read), per draft
length and for steps with and without prefill, with their mean context: what
the cost model still misses (the cut arm had ~4 ms per step at C=256 that the
KV read does not explain).
